OpenWrt configuration export control (2026-09-25)
===============================================

The dedicated OpenWrt gateway page now offers Download Config to a signed-in
super administrator with controller:write. The existing authorization store
recognizes its explicit superuser flag when checking that permission. Other
roles, absent users and super administrators without that permission do not see
the action. These are presentation checks: the configuration-download API remains
the authority for role, permission, tenant/site scope and sensitive-data access.

The action uses the existing configuration-download hook and validation helper.
Opening the page does not fetch the archive. While a request is pending the
button is disabled; duplicate activation is refused. Navigation or unmounting
cancels delivery, and a late result cannot download the old gateway's archive.
Provider diagnostics do not appear in failure messages. Successful responses
preserve the original binary bytes and use the existing fixed filename. The
archive is not stored in query or mutation caches by this action.

This adds no backup storage, retention, history entry or restore operation. A
configuration export may contain credentials and other secrets. Protect the
downloaded file as sensitive administrator data. Browser download history and
the user's local filesystem are outside the application's retention controls.
The existing Sync control and other vendors' page branches are unchanged.

Fifteen focused component cases reproduce nine missing-action failures on the
old page and retain six access/existing-behavior controls. They use the actual
OpenWrt page and tab, with synthetic identity, API and browser-file facilities.
The initial full test run passed but TypeScript rejected three test-query options;
that failure was retained and the test-only correction was checked again.

Separate Chromium checks exercise this actual OpenWrt render branch through
Axios and a real loopback HTTP server: byte-exact downloads, current/legacy
scope metadata, refusal/error cases, duplicate suppression, navigation/unmount
and role visibility. Those responses and identities are synthetic. They do not
qualify login, API authorization, vendor transport, hardware or archive restore.
Full suite and browser counts are recorded in source-bound qualification receipts.

Backend source is unchanged from the separately qualified CGI transport work.
See OPENWRT-CONFIG-TRANSPORT for package/ACL requirements, size/deadline limits,
gzip-validation scope and remaining physical-device acceptance work. Its browser
checks originally exercised the generic gateway page; this work closes the
dedicated OpenWrt page's missing control. Production release qualification still
requires the complete shipping, security and operational acceptance gates.
